# Writes Go Through the Server-Time RPC

`updateTimerState` calls the **`update_timer_with_server_time` RPC**, not a
plain table `update`. The RPC sets `updated_at_ms` from the DB's clock, so all
clients agree on when the change happened — even if their local clocks drift.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — client clock leaks into updated_at_ms
await supabase.from('timers').update({ start_timestamp: now }).eq('id', id);

// ✅ GOOD — server stamps updated_at_ms
await supabase.rpc('update_timer_with_server_time', { ... });
```

## Optimistic local update

The client sets `updatedAtMs` **optimistically** using `getAdjustedNow()` so
the UI updates immediately, then lets the realtime event arrive and correct
it. Don't await the RPC before updating local state — that adds a perceptible
lag on slow networks.

When adding a new timer field that should sync, extend this RPC (and its DB
function) rather than introducing a second write path.
